# A150 — the event stream's head is built like every other response (2026-09-16)

## What the stream path did
# `HTTPServer.respond` assembled the server-sent-events head by hand:
#     let head =
#         "HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\n"
#         + "Content-Type: text/event-stream; charset=utf-8\r\n"
#         + "Cache-Control: no-store\r\n"
#         + "X-Accel-Buffering: no\r\n"
#         + "Connection: keep-alive\r\n"
#         + "\r\n"
# so the one response that stays open carried none of the headers `HTTPResponse.serialised` adds —
# `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`, `X-Frame-Options: DENY` and the
# content-security policy. A76 fixed the serialiser; this path never went through it.

## The fix
# `HTTPResponse` now builds every head in one private `head(keepAlive:contentLength:)`:
# `serialised` appends the body and passes its length, and `streamingHead()` passes no length and appends
# nothing, which is what a stream needs. The stream's own response is `HTTPResponse.eventStream()`, so
# its content type and its `X-Accel-Buffering: no` hint are declared in the same place as every other
# response's headers rather than in the wire text, and the route sends
# `HTTPResponse.eventStream().streamingHead()`.

## The tests, against both behaviours
# `AuditS1SecurityHeaderTests` (A76's suite, which owns the live-server harness) gains two:
# the stream head compared header-by-header with a complete response's head, and the live
# `GET /api/events` read over a real socket.

$ swift test --filter AuditS1SecurityHeaderTests          # with the fix
  ✔ Test "The event stream's head carries the same security headers as every other response"
  ✔ Test "The live event stream answers with those headers"
  ✔ Test run with 10 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.058 seconds.

$ swift test --filter AuditS1SecurityHeaderTests          # with the hand-written head put back
  ✔ Test "The event stream's head carries the same security headers as every other response"
  ✘ Test "The live event stream answers with those headers"
      Expectation failed: http.value(forHTTPHeaderField: "X-Content-Type-Options") == "nosniff"
      Expectation failed: http.value(forHTTPHeaderField: "Referrer-Policy") == "no-referrer"
      Expectation failed: http.value(forHTTPHeaderField: "X-Frame-Options") == "DENY"
      Expectation failed: policy.contains("default-src 'none'")   (got "")
# The first test passes under the mutation on purpose: it checks the head the response type produces,
# which is still correct — the *live* test is the one that sees what the server actually wrote, and it
# fails on all four headers. That division is the point of having both.

# Notes on the way. `URLSession.data(for:)` waits for the whole body, and a stream's body never ends, so
# the first version of the live test sat there for the session's 60-second timeout; `bytes(for:)` returns
# the head as soon as it arrives. And the header comparison skips exactly two lines — `Content-Length`
# and `Content-Type` — because a stream has no length and a content type of its own; everything else must
# match, which is what makes this a drift guard rather than a snapshot.

